WORKING RULES
Implement the project, not merely a plan or mockup.
Inspect the workspace, report definitions and installed tools first.
Preserve unrelated files and never modify the semantic model without approval.
Reuse suitable existing code and dependencies.
Ask only for genuinely blocking information or required installation consent.
Never request, display or store credentials in chat, source files or reports.
Do not claim success until the relevant checks have actually passed.
Use synthetic fixtures only for explicitly labelled tests.

PREREQUISITES AND INSTALLATION
Check Windows, Power BI Desktop, a supported Node.js LTS version, npm,
Python, a project virtual environment, ADOMD.NET/model-access dependencies,
Power BI visual tooling and browser-test tooling.
Install missing project dependencies and generate dependency lockfiles.
Before system-wide changes, explain what will be installed and obtain
required approval. Never bypass organizational restrictions or elevation.
Use the actual selected Python interpreter, not an assumed PATH executable.
Provide repeatable install, build, start, stop and diagnostic commands.

ARCHITECTURE
Create:
1. A TypeScript Power BI custom visual, distributed as .pbiviz.
2. A local Python companion that queries the open Desktop semantic model.
3. Automated tests and documentation.

If BOTH versions are requested, create distinct visual GUIDs, package names
and localhost ports. Keep the local-only version independent of AI calls.
Share suitable UI and chart code without mixing their data-routing behavior.

Use maintained libraries such as Chart.js and Lucide.
Query the Power BI semantic model, not the original Excel/database directly,
so existing measures and relationships determine results.

MODEL CONNECTION
Discover the currently open report by its exact name and resolve its
current Analysis Services port and catalog. Do not hardcode session ports
or silently select a different report.
Handle report reopening, unavailable models and ambiguous matches clearly.
Read visible measures, columns, types, descriptions, format strings and
relevant relationships. Avoid exposing unnecessary connection details.
Execute read-only DAX with bounded timeouts and result sizes.

LOCAL-ONLY VERSION
Require no AI model, provider API key or outbound AI requests.
Support schema-driven query-builder controls and documented exact-name
question patterns.
Reject ambiguous or unsupported requests rather than guessing.
Return charts, data and the exact executed DAX.

COPILOT AI VERSION
Build this only when cloud processing is authorized.
Use the official standalone GitHub Copilot CLI and its own authenticated
account. Do not extract VS Code credentials or reuse the current chat.
Verify authentication with a harmless prompt containing no report data.
Guide the user through official login when needed.
Use an available model or automatic selection; do not invent model IDs.
No separate provider API key should be necessary.

Inspect the installed CLI’s current flags. Use structured JSON output,
disable custom instructions and MCP servers, and explicitly restrict tools.
Verify zero available tools before sending report information.
Do not assume that a value-less tool flag disables tools.
Run in an isolated working directory and suppress child console windows.
Document Copilot usage limits and possible CLI/cloud session retention.

BOUNDED AI WORKFLOW
- Interpret the question using verified schema and limited conversation history.
- Generate a structured plan, not arbitrary executable DAX or code.
- Validate every measure, column, filter and operation.
- Construct DAX locally from approved templates.
- Execute locally with limits.
- Allow at most one repair attempt.
- Ask for clarification if the request cannot be represented correctly.
- Never silently discard requested conditions.
- Explain results using bounded, explicitly disclosed evidence.
- Preserve valid query results if AI explanation fails.

Do not enable shell execution, file editing, web browsing or autonomous
model modifications through the chatbot.

SECURITY AND BACKGROUND OPERATION
Bind companions only to loopback addresses.
Protect schema/query routes with expiring, unpredictable session tokens.
Use a native Windows approval dialog with a verification code matching
the visual. No typed pairing passphrase should be necessary.
Do not trust a null browser origin as proof of identity.
Validate hosts, origins, request sizes, payloads and allowed operations.

If authorized, install per-user background startup without administrator
privileges. Supervise exited services, prevent duplicate instances and
use bounded restart intervals and rotating operational logs.
Do not log secrets, prompts or result data by default.
Use windowless Python and suppress PowerShell/Copilot child console windows.
Keep intentional approval dialogs visible.
Provide disable/uninstall instructions.
Explain that startup does not eliminate session approval, authentication
requirements or model availability constraints.

WELCOME SCREEN
Create a polished welcome message appropriate to the business domain.
Show six clickable starter questions grounded in the actual model.
Include a meeting-summary starter and five useful chart questions.
Do not hardcode retail fields or assume unsupported metrics.
Validate the starters against live data before declaring them ready.
Choose compatible initial chart types for each starter.

MEETING SUMMARY
Support requests such as “Give me a complete report summary for the meeting.”

Generate a multi-panel briefing, not a single chart or text paragraph.
Select approximately six useful analytical views from verified metadata,
business context and available comparison measures.
Possible sections include:
- Executive KPIs
- Time trends and valid prior-period comparisons
- Category/product contribution
- Store/region/team contribution
- Channel/customer segmentation
- Margin, efficiency, retention or another available business measure

Do not fabricate missing measures, targets or benchmarks.
Each panel must include:
- A descriptive title
- An appropriate chart
- A concise observation based on its executed results
- Data and executed-DAX tabs
- Relevant coverage or truncation notes

Include an executive narrative, measured improvements, measured declines,
suggested investigations and coverage gaps.
Calculate numerical findings deterministically where practical.
Distinguish contribution from performance against a target.
Do not invent causal explanations or assume that lower values are worse.
Check comparison definitions, zero denominators, missing values and
partial periods. If evidence is insufficient, say so.
Preserve successful sections when another fails and mark partial coverage.

Clearly distinguish a configured semantic-model briefing from an exhaustive
review of every report page. Explicitly support or reject requested summary
filters; never silently ignore them.
In AI mode, send only bounded, authorized evidence for narrative generation.

UI AND CHARTS
Use a refined, light, business-focused interface inspired by conversational
analytics products, without copying proprietary branding.
Apply the configured accent color with readable contrast and restrained
supporting colors.

Include:
- Welcome message and six starter questions
- Conversation history and new-conversation control
- Connection status and matching-code approval
- Guided query builder with measure, optional second measure,
  grouping and typed equality filters
- Loading, cancellation, clarification and recovery states
- Chart, Data and DAX tabs
- Copyable DAX
- Clear cloud-processing and filter-scope notices

Support bar, horizontal bar, line, area, donut, scatter and KPI views.
Offer only compatible chart types:
- Donuts require a suitable complete, non-negative part-to-whole series.
- Scatter requires two numeric measures and must handle missing pairs.
- KPI cards require meaningful aggregate values.
- Time charts must use a valid chronological ordering.
Respect model number/currency/percentage formats rather than guessing.
Do not misrepresent the first N rows as a top-N ranking.
Changing chart type must not change the underlying returned result.

Charts remain inside the custom visual; do not describe them as separately
created native Power BI report visuals.

Ensure responsive layouts, stable chart dimensions, accessible labels,
keyboard operation, visible focus, readable long labels, clear empty states
and reduced-motion support. Avoid overlapping text or nested decorative cards.

TRANSPARENCY
Do not promise perfect accuracy or unrestricted natural-language support.
Do not claim that report slicers automatically affect backend queries.
Either implement verified filter propagation or display the limitation.
Do not equate Desktop author access with Power BI Service-user RLS.
Explain which components run locally and which use the cloud.
Do not label the solution production-ready merely because it packages.

VERIFICATION
Add focused tests for:
- Schema and plan validation
- Unsupported-operation rejection
- Query repair limits
- Authorization and token expiry
- Background restart and duplicate prevention
- Hidden subprocess execution
- Summary calculations, missing sections and narrative failures

Use Playwright to verify both wide and narrow layouts, all compatible
chart modes, nonblank chart canvases, welcome starters, multi-panel briefs,
Data/DAX tabs and error states. Inspect screenshots.

Test every starter and at least one complete briefing against the live model.
Compare selected results with direct local DAX queries.
Separate mocked browser tests from live integration evidence.
Check dependency advisories and report unresolved production risks.
If Desktop host interaction requires the user, provide exact steps and
leave that requirement explicitly unverified.

FINAL DELIVERY
Provide:
- Complete source code and dependency lockfiles
- Repeatable installation and Windows startup scripts
- Versioned .pbiviz packages
- Local preview URL(s)
- Exact Power BI import and connection instructions
- README covering architecture, permissions, cloud data handling,
  supported questions, summary scope, limits and troubleshooting
- Test results distinguishing passed, failed and unverified checks
- A short video-demo checklist using validated questions

Implement and verify until complete or genuinely blocked.
When blocked, identify the precise cause and the smallest necessary user
action. Never substitute a mockup for a working integration without clearly
labelling it.
